      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "Safinamide has absolute oral bioavailability of about 95% (90% CI 90–99%) versus IV in healthy subjects, demonstrating that a small-molecule oral drug with aromatic amine/amide functionality can be almost completely absorbed.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "Tolfenamic acid, an N-aryl anthranilic acid sharing the N-aryl aniline motif, has absolute oral bioavailability of 63.2% in healthy volunteers, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Nicotinic acid, a pyridine-containing small molecule, has systemic availability of about 90%, supporting high oral bioavailability for the pyridine-containing query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C52"],
      "claim": "Bumetanide, a sulfonamide/aniline loop diuretic, has absolute bioavailability of 0.90 in normal subjects, supporting high F for the sulfonylurea/aniline query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C53"],
      "claim": "Isoniazid, a pyridine-containing drug, has absolute bioavailability of 93% in fasting TB patients, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C43"],
      "claim": "Isradipine has low absolute oral bioavailability (16%) in healthy subjects, but this reflects dihydropyridine first-pass metabolism and is not applicable to the sulfonylurea/pyridine query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a pyridine sulfonylurea (torsemide-like). High-transfer analogues with relevant aromatic amine, pyridine, and sulfonamide features consistently show high absolute oral bioavailability (safinamide 95%, bumetanide 90%, isoniazid 93%, nicotinic acid 90%, tolfenamic acid 63%). The only low-F analogue, isradipine, is a dihydropyridine with extensive first-pass metabolism and is mechanistically distinct. Thus F >=20% is strongly supported.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute bioavailability measurement for the exact query molecule in the provided evidence set.",
    "No query-specific data on first-pass metabolism or transporter effects, though the high-F analogues mitigate this uncertainty."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

